  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative self-reporting from a sample of 52 practitioners, which may not fully capture the technical performance or objective usage rates of the digital tools.",
      "what": "This study examines the awareness, adoption, drivers, and barriers of digital planning tools through qualitative interviews with 52 environmental planning practitioners across eight regions of Ghana. The findings reveal that while awareness of digital technologies is high, actual adoption is low and heavily concentrated on a narrow set of tools, specifically drones and artificial intelligence.",
      "why": "It identifies a critical gap between technology awareness and actual implementation in sub-Saharan African planning departments, demonstrating that public-private partnerships are the most vital driver for technology adoption due to severe constraints in government capacity."
    }
